## GitHub repositories

Every `github_repository` entry of `resources` needs a GitHub token, public repositories included: the API rejects one sent without a token with a 400, on create and on every update. Do not put it in `resources` as `authorization_token`: `resources` is not sensitive, so the token would appear in plan output and in saved plan files and state. The provider rejects that configuration. Set the token in the write-only `repository_authorization_tokens_wo` map instead, keyed by the repository `url` exactly as written in `resources`. Terraform never stores it in the plan or the state, and the API never returns it. Write-only arguments require Terraform 1.11 or later.

```terraform
resource "anthropic_deployment" "repository" {
  name           = "repository-deployment"
  agent_id       = anthropic_agent.assistant.id
  environment_id = anthropic_environment.sessions.id
  initial_events = jsonencode([
    { type = "user.message", content = [{ type = "text", text = "Review the open pull requests." }] }
  ])

  resources = jsonencode([
    { type = "github_repository", url = "https://github.com/example-org/private-repo" }
  ])

  repository_authorization_tokens_wo = {
    "https://github.com/example-org/private-repo" = var.github_token
  }
  repository_authorization_tokens_wo_version = 1
}
```

Terraform cannot detect a change to a write-only value: increment `repository_authorization_tokens_wo_version` to send a new token. Like any update, a rotation also re-pins the deployment to the latest version of its agent (see `agent_id`).

The provider sends the tokens with every update, since each update resends `resources` and the API requires the tokens there. Keep a token in the configuration for each repository: a plan that creates or updates the deployment fails on a `github_repository` without one (at apply, when `resources` is only known then), and a token value that is null or empty is always rejected. This also applies after `terraform import`: set the tokens before the first update. A plan without a change, a refresh and a destroy send no `resources`, so they work without the tokens.

Error messages never show a key of `repository_authorization_tokens_wo`, since the map is sensitive: a key that matches no repository is reported by count, next to the repository URLs found in `resources`.

## Claude Platform on AWS

The gateway serves `/v1/deployments`, although its [route table](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/claude-platform-on-aws-iam-actions) does not list it.

There, each run creates its session as an IAM role of your AWS account ([scheduled deployments](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/scheduled-deployments)). Creating a deployment for an agent whose execution identity is not an `aws_role` fails with a 400: `On Claude Platform on AWS a deployment runs as the agent's AWS role. This agent has no execution_identity of type aws_role, so the deployment could never run.` The role's trust policy must let the `aws-external-anthropic.amazonaws.com` service principal call `sts:AssumeRole` and `sts:SetSourceIdentity`, the role needs `aws-external-anthropic:CreateSession`, `sts:GetWebIdentityToken` and `sts:TagGetWebIdentityToken`, and whoever sets the role on the agent needs `iam:PassRole` on it, in the same account. Set it in the agent's `execution_identity` (see `anthropic_agent`).

